{{ElectionsSC}}
The '''2002 South Carolina gubernatorial election''' was held on November 5, 2002, to select the [[Governor of South Carolina|governor]] of the state of [[South Carolina]]. [[Mark Sanford]], the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] nominee, defeated incumbent [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] governor [[Jim Hodges]] to become the [[List of governors of South Carolina|115th governor]] of South Carolina. Hodges became only the third incumbent governor and the first Democratic governor in South Carolina history to lose re-election. With Sanford's election, Republicans won a trifecta in the state for the first time since 1877.

==Democratic primary==
[[Governor of South Carolina|Governor]] [[Jim Hodges]] faced no opposition from [[South Carolina Democratic Party|South Carolina Democrats]] and avoided a [[Partisan primary|primary election]].

==Republican primary==
The [[South Carolina Republican Party]] held their [[Partisan primary|primary]] on June 11, 2002, and the runoff on June 25, 2002. The contest became a race between [[Lieutenant Governor of South Carolina|Lieutenant Governor]] [[Bob Peeler]] from [[the Upstate]] and [[Mark Sanford]], a former [[United States House of Representatives|representative]] of the [[South Carolina's 1st congressional district|1st congressional district]] in the [[South Carolina Low Country|Lowcountry]]. Sanford received the support of the candidates eliminated from the runoff election and easily defeated Peeler.

